26. Bull Case

The Bull Case develops as follows:

Professional-sport overload continues increasing.
Player availability becomes more economically valuable.
Clubs invest more in workload management, injury prevention and AI.
Sony integrates STATSports, Hawk-Eye and KinaTrax technically.
Shared data models connect training, matches and biomechanics.
Sony develops decision systems rather than only dashboards.
Clubs and leagues become deeply integrated with the infrastructure.
Federated or anonymized learning improves model quality.
Sony monetizes related data through broadcast, gaming and fan products.
Recurring software and data revenue increases.
Sports-Tech becomes a material corporate pillar.

In this scenario, Sony could control one of the most valuable data and decision infrastructures in global sports.

27. Base Case

In the Base Case:

integration occurs only partially,
shared products are launched,
league and club contracts expand,
cross-selling improves,
recurring revenue rises,
but no complete global operating system emerges.

Sports-Tech becomes a profitable growth business but remains much smaller than Sony’s major entertainment and imaging segments.

This would improve Sony’s quality and diversification without necessarily causing a major revaluation.

28. Bear Case

In the Bear Case:

the businesses remain organizationally fragmented,
common data models do not emerge,
clubs prevent meaningful cross-organization benchmarking,
data rights block the strongest network effects,
Catapult retains superior access to coaching workflows,
outside AI providers control the decision layer,
Sony remains primarily a hardware and raw-data provider,
margins remain limited,
and the business stays immaterial to the group.

In this case, the Sports-Tech thesis has largely failed.

Any Sony investment must then be justified entirely by the core businesses.

29. Principal Risks
Integration Risk

Several acquisitions may remain a portfolio rather than becoming a platform.

Data-Rights Risk

Sony may operate the infrastructure without obtaining rights to train shared models or commercialize derived insights.

Trust Risk

Clubs may resist concentrating medical, tactical and physical information with one large corporation.

League-Club Conflict

A strong league relationship may not create access to daily club workflows.

Competitive Risk

Catapult, specialized AI companies, internal club systems and other tracking providers may control the highest-value layers.

AI Commoditization

Generic models may become interchangeable.

Value may then concentrate in data, workflow access and distribution.

However, standardized data may also allow independent AI providers to capture the decision layer.

Hardware and Service Intensity

The business may have lower margins and higher implementation costs than expected.

Group Dilution

Even strong growth may remain invisible inside a large company for many years.

Capital-Allocation Risk

Sony may continue acquiring businesses without earning sufficient returns.

Customer Concentration

Major leagues and federations may possess substantial bargaining power.

Regulation

Health, performance and biometric data may face increasingly strict requirements.

Technological Substitution

Camera-based tracking may replace some wearable applications, or wearables may retain advantages unavailable to cameras.

30. Kill Criteria

The Sports-Tech thesis becomes materially weaker if:

Sony releases no meaningful joint products over several years,
STATSports, Hawk-Eye and KinaTrax remain operationally separate,
combined customer contracts do not appear,
league-wide platform contracts do not expand,
no AI decision products emerge,
sports-business growth slows materially,
recurring software revenue remains limited,
cross-selling remains absent,
Catapult clearly wins technologically and commercially,
clubs increasingly build their own internal systems,
data rights prevent shared models,
acquisitions continue without integration,
or the business remains immaterial after years of investment.

A thesis without kill criteria can reinterpret every failure as temporary.

These criteria prevent strategic imagination from becoming unfalsifiable.

31. Confirmation Signals

The thesis gains evidence if Sony:

begins disclosing sports revenue or profit,
combines STATSports, Hawk-Eye and KinaTrax information in one product,
releases common APIs or data standards,
