38. General Principle Derived from the Sony Case

The broader principle is:

A company’s future platform may first become visible not through financial reporting, but through the pattern of assets management repeatedly acquires, retains and attempts to connect.

An insignificant product is usually irrelevant.

Several related insignificant products deserve attention.

Several complementary products may indicate an emerging system.

A large parent company can provide that system with:

capital,
distribution,
customer relationships,
technical infrastructure,
brand,
and patience.

The resulting investment method is not simply to find ecosystems.

It is to recognize them before they are universally recognized as ecosystems.

The investor therefore studies not only reported business segments, but also:

acquisition sequences,
capital-allocation patterns,
shared customers,
technical interfaces,
organizational consolidation,
distribution channels,
and the missing components of a possible future architecture.

The final principle is:

Markets frequently price existing products. Long-term alpha may come from recognizing emerging architectures.





